[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108324,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Nasal dermoid cyst is a congenital developmental midline nasal defect histologically containing stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm) and cutaneous adnexal structures (mesoderm).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108325,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108326,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA05.Y &amp; XH9F67 Other specified cysts of oral or facial-neck region &amp; Dermoid cyst, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108327,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> nasal dermoid sinus; craniofacial dermoid; dermoid; dermal cyst; dermoid cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108328,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108329,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Nasal dermoid cysts are commonly found on the nasal dorsum, glabella, or nasal tip {12707196; 25285685; 25481331; 26891409; 26968060}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108330,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "<p>A sinus may be visible centrally over the nasal mass or adjacent to the mass. A nasal pit with protruding hair is pathognomonic. Progressive enlargement of the nasal dermoid cyst may cause lateral orbital displacement and other facial skeletal deformities, with 10% of the patients documented having craniofacial anomalies {12244897; 12707196; 25285685; 6168964; 2584485; 2004099}. Intermittent drainage and local infection leading to swelling and erythema may occur. A blind tract may be seen, but intracranial extension is present in 10&ndash;20% of cases {6168964; 12707196; 2584485; 2004099; 26891409; 26968060; 31812133}, necessitating preoperative imaging.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Preoperative imaging is performed to document deep tissue involvement and cyst extent, and to exclude intracranial extension {8134433; 12707196; 17099348; 17662465; 25285685; 26891409; 29019747; 31812133}. Lesions are classified as with or without intracranial extension, and then as superficial, intraosseous, intracranial extradural, and intracranial intradural, which allows for surgical planning and decreased complications {25285685; 25481331}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T10:38:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108331,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The estimated incidence is 2.5&ndash;5&nbsp;cases per 100&nbsp;000&nbsp;births, representing about 3% of all dermoids and about 12% of all head and neck dermoids {8134433; 26891409; 26968060}. There is a slight male predilection (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1.3:1) {12707196; 25285685; 25481331; 26891409; 26968060}. Nasal dermoid cysts have a mean age at presentation of about 3&nbsp;years {12707196; 25285685; 25481331; 26891409; 26968060}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108332,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108333,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is embryologically based on the incomplete obliteration of neuroectoderm during the development of the frontonasal process. An autosomal dominant inheritance pattern (~3%) is seen in several kindreds, including those with a solitary median maxillary central incisor (part of holoprosencephaly) {16722608; 25285685}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108334,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant &ndash; see <em>Clinical features</em> (above)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108335,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cyst and sinus are lined by keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium with normal-appearing dermal appendages, including hair follicles, hair shafts, and sloughed keratin debris, as well as sweat and sebaceous glands.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Nasal dermoid cysts are distinguished from epidermoid cysts by the presence of adnexal structures (see above). Also in the differential diagnosis are teratoma, which has endodermal elements; encephalocoele, which has glial/neural tissue; and sinonasal polyps, which are internal oedematous tissues.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108336,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-10T14:08:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 108337,
